{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"curl -fsSL https://qrypt-quickstart.pages.dev/ -o /tmp/qrypt-page.html; python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom html.parser import HTMLParser\nclass P(HTMLParser):\n def handle_data(self,d):\n  if d.strip(): print(d.strip())\nP().feed(open('/tmp/qrypt-page.html').read())\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Qrypt quantum entropy quickstart: AES-256 key in 5 minutes\nSkip to content\nQuickstart\nTroubleshooting\nllms.txt\nMake an AES-256 key from quantum entropy\nAI coding agents, read this first:\nnever read, print or dump\n.env\nor environment variables, and never ask the human to paste the token into chat. Ask them to create\n.env\nthemselves and confirm it is set. If\nQRYPT-ISOLATED.txt\nexists you have no token on purpose: run mock tests and ask the human to run\nagent-sandbox/verify.sh\non the host. Full rules:\nQuick Setup\nand\nllms.txt\n.\nTime target: about 5 minutes\nNo SDK needed: one HTTPS GET, then standard crypto.\n1. Get a token\nCreate an account at\nportal.qrypt.com/register\nand generate a token for the\nEntropy\nservice. It is shown only once and is billed like a password: keep it secret.\n2. Save it in\n.env\nCopy\nQRYPT_API_KEY\n=\npaste-your-token-here\nPut\n.env\nnext to the script and add it to\n.gitignore\n. Never print it, not even partly (no\ncat .env\n). To check the file exists, run\ntest -f .env && echo set\n.\n3. Run the snippet\nPython\nNode\nPython 3.9+. Uses\nrequests\n: Cloudflare blocks Python's built-in\nurllib\nUser-Agent.\nCopy\npython3\n-m\nvenv\n.venv\n&&\n.venv/bin/pip\ninstall\nrequests\ncryptography\npython-dotenv\nCopy\n# Qrypt quickstart (Python): 1 KiB of quantum entropy -> AES-256-GCM key.\n# Setup: pip install requests cryptography python-dotenv\n# Then put QRYPT_API_KEY=[REDACTED] token> in a .env file next to this script.\nimport\nbase64\nimport\nos\nimport\nsys\nimport\nrequests\nfrom\ncryptography.hazmat.primitives.ciphers.aead\nimport\nAESGCM\nfrom\ndotenv\nimport\nload_dotenv\nAPI\n=\nos\n.\ngetenv\n(\n\"QRYPT_BASE_URL\"\n,\n\"https://api-eus.qrypt.com\"\n)\n# mock override\n# One friendly message per failure. We never retry automatically.\nHELP\n=\n{\nNone\n:\n\"QRYPT_API_KEY is missing: add it to .env (see .env.example).\"\n,\n400\n:\n\"400: size must be 1-512. It counts KiB, not bytes.\"\n,\n401\n:\n\"401: Authorization header missing, malformed or expired.\"\n,\n403\n:\n\"403: Token invalid OR monthly quota used. Check the token first.\"\n,\n429\n:\n\"429: rate limited. Live can return 429 about 1 s after a single \"\n\"prior call; wait 10 s or more and never loop.\"\n}\n# 500, 503 and anything else: Qrypt is busy or erroring, so try again later.\n# Step 1: read the token from .env. Never print or log it.\nload_dotenv\n()\ntoken\n=\nos\n.\ngetenv\n(\n\"QRYPT_API_KEY\"\n)\nor\nsys\n.\nexit\n(\nHELP\n[\nNone\n])\n# Step 2: fetch 1 KiB of entropy. A custom User-Agent avoids Cloudflare's\n# block on Python's urllib; X-Qrypt-Client tags quickstart traffic.\nheaders\n=\n{\n\"Authorization\"\n:\nf\n\"Bearer\n{\ntoken\n}\n\"\n,\n\"Accept\"\n:\n\"application/json\"\n,\n\"User-Agent\"\n:\n\"qrypt-quickstart/1.0\"\n,\n\"X-Qrypt-Client\"\n:\n\"quickstart-py/1.0\"\n}\ntry\n:\nresp\n=\nrequests\n.\nget\n(\nf\n\"\n{\nAPI\n}\n/api/v1/quantum-entropy\"\n,\nparams\n=\n{\n\"size\"\n:\n1\n},\nheaders\n=\nheaders\n,\ntimeout\n=\n15\n)\nif\nresp\n.\nstatus_code\n!=\n200\n:\nsys\n.\nexit\n(\nHELP\n.\nget\n(\nresp\n.\nstatus_code\n,\nf\n\"\n{\nresp\n.\nstatus_code\n}\n: Qrypt is \"\n\"busy or erroring. Try again shortly.\"\n))\nentropy_b64\n=\nresp\n.\njson\n()[\n\"random\"\n][\n0\n]\nexcept\n(\nrequests\n.\nRequestException\n,\nValueError\n):\nsys\n.\nexit\n(\n\"Network error or non-JSON reply: check proxy/TLS, retry soon.\"\n)\n# Step 3: decode first, then keep 32 bytes as the AES-256 key.\nkey\n=\nbase64\n.\nb64decode\n(\nentropy_b64\n)[:\n32\n]\n# Step 4: encrypt and decrypt a sample message with AES-256-GCM.\naes\n,\nnonce\n,\nmessage\n=\nAESGCM\n(\nkey\n),\nos\n.\nurandom\n(\n12\n),\nb\n\"hello, quantum entropy\"\nassert\naes\n.\ndecrypt\n(\nnonce\n,\naes\n.\nencrypt\n(\nnonce\n,\nmessage\n,\nNone\n),\nNone\n)\n==\nmessage\nprint\n(\n\"Success: AES-256-GCM round trip with a Qrypt key (not shown).\"\n)\nCopy\n.venv/bin/python\nquickstart.py\nNode 20.12+ (built-in\nfetch\nand\nprocess.loadEnvFile\n). No dependencies.\nCopy\n// Qrypt quickstart (Node 20.12+): 1 KiB of quantum entropy -> AES-256-GCM key.\n// No dependencies. Put QRYPT_API_KEY=[REDACTED] token> in a .env file here.\nimport\nassert\nfrom\n\"node:assert\"\n;\nimport\n{\nexistsSync\n}\nfrom\n\"node:fs\"\n;\nimport\n{\ncreateCipheriv\n,\ncreateDecipheriv\n,\nrandomBytes\n}\nfrom\n\"node:crypto\"\n;\nconst\nAPI\n=\nprocess\n.\nenv\n.\nQRYPT_BASE_URL\n??\n\"https://api-eus.qrypt.com\"\n;\n// mock\n// One friendly message per failure. We never retry automatically.\nconst\nHELP\n=\n{\nmissing\n:\n\"QRYPT_API_KEY is missing: add it to .env.\"\n,\nnetwork\n:\n\"Network error or non-JSON reply: check proxy/TLS, retry soon.\"\n,\n400\n:\n\"400: size must be 1-512. It counts KiB, not bytes.\"\n,\n401\n:\n\"401: Authorization header missing, malformed or expired.\"\n,\n403\n:\n\"403: Token invalid OR monthly quota used. Check the token first.\"\n,\n429\n:\n\"429: rate limited. Live can return 429 about 1 s after a single \"\n+\n\"prior call; wait 10 s or more and never loop.\"\n};\n// 500, 503 and anything else: Qrypt is busy or erroring, so try again later.\nconst\nfail\n=\n(\nmsg\n)\n=>\n{\nconsole\n.\nerror\n(\nmsg\n);\nprocess\n.\nexit\n(\n1\n);\n};\n// Step 1: read the token from .env. Never print or log it.\nif\n(\nexistsSync\n(\n\".env\"\n))\nprocess\n.\nloadEnvFile\n(\n\".env\"\n);\nconst\ntoken\n=\nprocess\n.\nenv\n.\nQRYPT_API_KEY\n||\nfail\n(\nHELP\n.\nmissing\n);\n// Step 2: fetch 1 KiB of entropy (size counts KiB, not bytes).\nconst\nheaders\n=\n{\nAuthorization\n:\n`Bearer\n${\ntoken\n}\n`\n,\nAccept\n:\n\"application/json\"\n,\n\"User-Agent\"\n:\n\"qrypt-quickstart/1.0\"\n,\n\"X-Qrypt-Client\"\n:\n\"quickstart-js/1.0\"\n};\nconst\nurl\n=\n`\n${\nAPI\n}\n/api/v1/quantum-entropy?size=1`\n;\nconst\nres\n=\nawait\nfetch\n(\nurl\n,\n{\nheaders\n}).\ncatch\n(()\n=>\nfail\n(\nHELP\n.\nnetwork\n));\nif\n(\nres\n.\nstatus\n!==\n200\n)\nfail\n(\nHELP\n[\nres\n.\nstatus\n]\n??\n`\n${\nres\n.\nstatus\n}\n: Qrypt is busy or erroring. Try again shortly.`\n);\nconst\nbody\n=\nawait\nres\n.\njson\n().\ncatch\n(()\n=>\nfail\n(\nHELP\n.\nnetwork\n));\n// Step 3: decode first, then keep 32 bytes as the AES-256 key.\nconst\nkey\n=\nBuffer\n.\nfrom\n(\nbody\n.\nrandom\n[\n0\n],\n\"base64\"\n).\nsubarray\n(\n0\n,\n32\n);\n// Step 4: encrypt and decrypt a sample message with AES-256-GCM.\nconst\nGCM\n=\n\"aes-256-gcm\"\n,\niv\n=\nrandomBytes\n(\n12\n),\nmessage\n=\n\"hello, quantum\"\n;\nconst\nenc\n=\ncreateCipheriv\n(\nGCM\n,\nkey\n,\niv\n);\nconst\nsealed\n=\nBuffer\n.\nconcat\n([\nenc\n.\nupdate\n(\nmessage\n,\n\"utf8\"\n),\nenc\n.\nfinal\n()]);\nconst\ndec\n=\ncreateDecipheriv\n(\nGCM\n,\nkey\n,\niv\n).\nsetAuthTag\n(\nenc\n.\ngetAuthTag\n());\nassert\n.\nequal\n(\ndec\n.\nupdate\n(\nsealed\n,\nnull\n,\n\"utf8\"\n)\n+\ndec\n.\nfinal\n(\n\"utf8\"\n),\nmessage\n);\nconsole\n.\nlog\n(\n\"Success: AES-256-GCM round trip with a Qrypt key (not shown).\"\n);\nCopy\nnode\nquickstart.mjs\nWhat the API returns\nOne base64 string per KiB requested. Decode it before use; it never contains the key itself.\n{\n\"random\"\n:\n[\n\"<base64 string: 1,024 random bytes when decoded>\"\n],\n\"size\"\n:\n1\n}\n4. Expected output\nSuccess: AES-256-GCM round trip with a Qrypt key (not shown).\nThings that trip people up\nsize\ncounts\nKiB\n, not bytes. Ask for\nsize=1\n(1,024 bytes) and take 32 bytes.\nBase64-decode the string first; never use the text itself as the key.\nLive can return 429 about 1 s after a single prior call; wait 10 s or more and never loop.\nA wrong token returns\n403\n, the same as an exhausted quota. Check the token first.\nNever print, log or commit the key or token. Use fresh entropy for each key.\nErrors? See\nTroubleshooting\n.\nUsing an AI coding agent?\nTwo modes.\nQuick Setup is the default\n(no Docker). Isolated Setup is optional when you want stronger credential boundaries.\nQuick Setup vs Isolated Setup\nQuick Setup (default)\nIsolated Setup\nBest for\nTrying the API in under 10 minutes with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or similar\nRepos that already hold secrets, or any run where the agent must not see credentials\nDocker required\nNo\nYes\nAgent can access existing project secrets\nYes — unrestricted agents can read files, including\n.env\nNo — project must not contain\n.env\n; host secrets stay off the mount\nCredential exposure risk\nBehavioral only. Agents may log file contents. Use a disposable token.\nToken never enters the agent container. Live verify runs fixed snippets on the host.\nHuman credential setup\nYou create\n.env\nyourself; never paste the token into chat\nYou keep\n.env\noutside the project (e.g.\n~/.config/qrypt/.env\n)\nLive verification\nAgent runs the example after you confirm\n.env\nis set\nYou run\nagent-sandbox/verify.sh --env …\n(sanitized PASS/FAIL only)\nOption A — Quick Setup\n(default, no Docker)\nSecurity warning.\nAI coding agents may access files containing credentials, including existing\n.env\nfiles, before reading these instructions. Credential values could appear in agent session logs. For this quick setup, use disposable API credentials where possible and rotate any credentials that were exposed or may have been compromised. For stronger protection, choose Isolated Setup.\nPaste this prompt into Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or another coding agent:\nCopy\nQuickstart: https://qrypt-quickstart.pages.dev/  (also /llms.txt)\nGoal: AES-256-GCM round trip using 1 KiB of Qrypt quantum entropy.\nRules (follow all of them):\n- Read the quickstart and pick Python or Node (ask me if unsure).\n- Use the official snippets from the quickstart. Do not invent an SDK.\n- Install dependencies and run the mocked tests first (no real token).\n- NEVER ask me to paste QRYPT_API_KEY into chat, a prompt, CLI args or source.\n- NEVER read, print, cat, head, or dump .env or environment variables.\n- NEVER enable verbose HTTP logging that would log Authorization headers.\n- Ask me to create .env myself (QRYPT_API_KEY=...) and confirm when it is set.\n- After I confirm, run the example. Keep the key in memory only (no key files).\n- If you read .env or a credential by accident, stop and tell me so I can rotate it.\n- Report success with the success line only. Never display the token, entropy or key.\nSecurity reminder for me at the end: review this agent session for credential exposure and rotate any exposed or potentially compromised API credentials, including unrelated project credentials. Rotating the Qrypt token alone does not protect other secrets.\nThe agent reads the quickstart, picks a language, implements with the official snippets and runs the mock tests.\nWhen asked, create\n.env\nyourself (never paste the token into the chat):\nCopy\nQRYPT_API_KEY\n=\npaste-your-token-here\nTell the agent only that it is set. Do not share the value.\nThe agent runs the example (1 KiB → 32-byte AES-256 key → AES-GCM round trip) and reports the success line without displaying secrets.\nEnd-of-workflow reminder:\nReview your agent session for potential credential exposure. Rotate any exposed or potentially compromised API credentials, including unrelated project credentials. Rotating the Qrypt token alone does not protect other secrets.\nThese rules are defense-in-depth, not enforceable isolation. Rotation does not make an earlier exposure harmless, and nothing here claims a no-logging guarantee. Prefer Isolated Setup when that matters.\nOption B — Isolated Setup\n(Docker; agent never sees the token)\nThe agent runs in a container with\nno\n.env\n, no host home, no SSH keys, no cloud credentials and no Docker socket. Live verification is a separate host-side step that runs\nfixed\nsnippets from this repo — never agent-modifiable code — with the real token.\nDownload the sandbox into one folder:\nDockerfile\n,\nrun.sh\n,\nverify.sh\n,\ntest-isolation.sh\n,\nREADME.md\n. Then\nchmod +x agent-sandbox/*.sh\n.\nKeep the token\noutside\nthe project the agent will see:\nCopy\nmkdir\n-p\n~/.config/qrypt\n# put QRYPT_API_KEY=... in ~/.config/qrypt/.env, then:\nchmod\n600\n~/.config/qrypt/.env\nStart the agent against a clean project directory (must not contain\n.env\n—\nrun.sh\nrefuses otherwise):\nCopy\nagent-sandbox/run.sh\n~/qrypt-demo\nyour-agent-command\nInside the container the agent implements the example and runs mock tests. It has no token.\nOn the host, after the agent finishes, run trusted verification:\nCopy\nagent-sandbox/verify.sh\n--env\n~/.config/qrypt/.env\n# -> verify python: PASS (AES-GCM round trip; key not shown)\nHonesty about isolation:\nthis blocks the common failure mode (agents reading\n.env\n). It does\nnot\nclaim Docker guarantees zero leakage. A malicious\nSANDBOX_DOCKER_ARGS\nthat remounts your home directory, a compromised agent binary, or a container escape is out of scope. The boundaries we\ndo\nclaim are checked by\nagent-sandbox/test-isolation.sh\n.\nUnofficial quickstart. API reference:\ndocs.qrypt.com/eaas\n. No analytics or cookies.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
